<h1>Exporting Test Batch for CI</h1>
<p>Everything configured in the controller so far lives in its session: the values in the run panels, the
	selected cases, the endpoints. A CI pipeline has no controller window, so it needs the same configuration in a
	form it can run on its own. <strong>Export for CI</strong> writes that form: a <strong>snapshot</strong> folder
	holding a <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/batches/">batch</a>, which is a framework file that lists the
	exported items with their configured values baked in, together with a launcher that runs the batch without any
	GUI and exits with a code a pipeline can gate on.</p>
<p>The export mirrors what the panel shows rather than copying the suite. For every exported suite, group and
	test it writes a small <strong>mirror</strong> class that subclasses the original and overrides only what was
	configured: which groups a suite runs, which tests a group runs, and the field values of each test. The suite's
	own code stays in its repository and is imported from there, so an export never edits or duplicates the tests.
	This is also the way an <a class="text-accent" href="/ai-agents/running/">AI agent</a> runs tests: it writes the
	same kind of file by hand.</p>

<h2 id="exporting-a-group">Exporting a group</h2>
<p>The button lives at the bottom of every run panel, next to <strong>Update Plan</strong> and
	<strong>Run</strong>, and it exports the scope the panel shows: one test from a test's panel, a group with its
	tests from a group's panel, and the whole session from the session row. Select the
	<strong>Statuses and Results</strong> group, whose five tests all have their folders set from the earlier
	pages:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/exporting-test-batch-for-ci/group-panel-export-button.png", "Run panel of the Statuses and Results group listing its five tests with their Cycles, Case Cycles and folder values, the panel of the first test beneath, and the Update Plan, Reset To Defaults, Export for CI and Run buttons at the bottom", "large") }}
<p>Click <strong class="text-accent">Export for CI</strong>. A folder dialog asks where to write the snapshot;
	choose an empty folder, here <code>C:\orcraft\ci</code>. The export validates the configuration the way
	<strong>Run</strong> does, so a missing required field stops it with the same message. When it is done, a
	message names the folder and counts the exported items. The folder holds the batch, the launcher and the
	mirrors:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('C:\\orcraft\\ci\\
  batch.py
  ci_run.py
  orcraft_test_suite_tutorial\\
    __init__.py
    test_suite_tutorial.py
    test_group_statuses_and_results\\
      __init__.py
      test_group_statuses_and_results.py', "text") }}
<p><code>batch.py</code> is the batch itself, a plain framework file with one item per exported top-level
	object. The group was exported as part of its suite, so the item is the suite, and its mirror narrows the suite
	to that one group:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('class BatchConfig(OrcraftTestBatchConfig):
	name = OrcraftConfigField.Text(default=\'ci\', label="Name")
	items = OrcraftConfigField.Items(default_factory=lambda: [
		OrcraftBatchItem(
			name=\'Test Suite: Orcraft Tutorial\',
			kind=\'suite\',
			source_path=\'orcraft_test_suite_tutorial/test_suite_tutorial.py\',
			class_name=\'TestSuiteTutorial\',
			import_roots=(\'.\', \'../orcraft-test-suite-tutorial\'),
			package_source_path=\'../orcraft-test-suite-tutorial\',
		),
	])') }}
<p><code>source_path</code> points at the mirror in the snapshot, <code>import_roots</code> puts the snapshot and
	the suite's own folder on the import path, and <code>package_source_path</code> names the suite folder that is
	<a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/packages-uploading/">uploaded to the endpoints</a>. The paths are
	relative to where the snapshot was written, which is why the suite has to stay where it was, or be pointed at
	again, when the snapshot runs elsewhere.</p>
<p>The suite mirror is a few lines. It imports the original config class, overrides its <code>groups</code> field
	with the one exported group, and marks itself as a mirror:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('from orcraft import OrcraftConfigField
from orcraft import OrcraftTestSuite
from test_suite_tutorial import TestSuiteTutorialConfig
from orcraft_test_suite_tutorial.test_group_statuses_and_results.test_group_statuses_and_results import TestGroupStatusesAndResults

class TestSuiteTutorialConfig(TestSuiteTutorialConfig):
	groups = OrcraftConfigField.Groups(default_factory=lambda: [TestGroupStatusesAndResults()])

class TestSuiteTutorial(OrcraftTestSuite):
	orcraft_ci_mirror = True

	def __init__(self):
		super().__init__(config=TestSuiteTutorialConfig())') }}
<p>The group mirror below it does the same for its five tests, and each test mirror redeclares the fields that
	were configured, such as the <strong>Maze Executable Folder</strong>, with the values from the panel. The
	<a class="text-accent" href="/framework/batches/#item-paths">framework reference</a> describes the item paths
	and the mirrors in full.</p>

<h2 id="running-the-snapshot">Running the snapshot</h2>
<p><code>ci_run.py</code> is the launcher. It runs the batch with no GUI, resolves where the suites are, and
	turns the outcome into an exit code. Its own header documents the command line:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('Usage:
    python ci_run.py [--suites-root DIR]

Suite-root resolution (first match wins), per item:
    1. --suites-root DIR         -> DIR/<repo_name>
    2. ORCRAFT_SUITE_ROOT_<REPO> -> that path (REPO is the upper-cased repo name)
    3. the relative path baked in at export time (works when the snapshot sits
       next to the original repos).

Exit codes: 0 = all passed, 10 = a failure, 20 = an error.', "text") }}
<p>Open a terminal in the snapshot folder and run it with the Python that has the <code>orcraft</code> package
	installed:</p>
{{ cmd.copyCommand("python ci_run.py") }}
<p>The batch starts its item in a subprocess, and the item's tests start together, exactly as they would under
	the controller. The console shows one line per test as it starts and finishes:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('Running 1 item in parallel subprocesses
	Test Suite: Orcraft Tutorial

