<p>The batch reports what the group's tests are built to produce: two passes, two failures and one error, from
	the <strong>Status: Error</strong> test that looks for a Maze version that does not exist. An error anywhere
	makes the batch an error, so the launcher exits with code 20; a run with failures and no errors exits with 10,
	and a clean run with 0. A pipeline step that runs <code>python ci_run.py</code> therefore fails exactly when
	the tests say it should, and the batch log written under the snapshot's <code>Logs</code> folder holds every
	test's log for the investigation.</p>

<h2 id="loading-the-batch-in-the-controller">Loading the batch in the controller</h2>
<p>A snapshot is not only for pipelines. <strong>Add Item</strong> in the controller opens <code>batch.py</code>
	like a suite file, and the batch appears in the tree with its items beneath it:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/exporting-test-batch-for-ci/batch-in-tree.png", "Execution tree with the original Orcraft Tutorial suite above and a Test Batch row named ci below it, holding a mirrored Test Suite Orcraft Tutorial row", "large") }}
<p>The <strong>Test Batch</strong> row named after the snapshot folder holds the mirrored suite, which in turn holds
	the exported group and its tests with the configured values already in place. It runs, plans and reports like
	any other row, so a snapshot that failed in a pipeline can be opened here, run again, and inspected in the
	<a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/details-dialog/">Details dialog</a> with the same values the pipeline
	used.</p>

<p>The snapshot pointed back at the suite's folder, and the controller keeps an eye on where that folder stands
	against its repository. The next page, <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/git-status-frame/">Git Status
	Frame</a>, shows the panel that does so.</p>
